<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barha Tegin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · Also known as Barhatakin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barha Tegin is named by the eleventh-century scholar al-Biruni as the founder of the Turk Shahi dynasty, which replaced the Nezak rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapisa">Kapisa</a> around 665. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-biruni">Al-Biruni</a> recorded traditions of a line of some sixty kings descended from him. The Turk Shahis ruled a largely Buddhist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> population until they were replaced by the Hindu Shahis in the ninth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barha-tegin.jpg" alt="Barha Tegin" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turk_Shahi_portrait._King_Sri_Ranasrikari._Late_7th_to_early_8th_century_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
